# =============================================================================
# Program      : 14_chain_of_custody_test.py
# Version      : 1.0
# Description  : Multi-Generation Chain-of-Custody Test (T-IFS expansion).
#
#                Every prior attack in this project applies ONE transform
#                (or one attack) to freshly-embedded audio and reports the
#                result. Real content doesn't have a one-hop lifecycle —
#                it gets compressed for one platform, re-shared, possibly
#                regenerated by AI at some point, re-compressed again,
#                shared further. The forensic question this raises, and
#                which nothing built so far answers, is: if a regeneration
#                event happens partway through a realistic processing
#                history, does it remain detectable after MORE benign
#                processing on top of it, or does the evidence get buried?
#                Conversely, does a long chain of PURELY benign processing
#                ever accumulate enough drift to look like a false
#                regeneration signal?
#
#                FIVE 4-HOP CHAINS, using the same fixed pool of benign
#                transforms (MP3@128kbps, resample@22050Hz, noise@30dB SNR,
#                gain@0.85 — moderate, representative settings, not a
#                battery sweep) and DAC (proven fast, full-clip real
#                regeneration) as the regeneration step, so results are
#                directly comparable across chains:
#                  all_benign    : compress -> resample -> noise -> gain
#                  regen_first   : DAC -> compress -> resample -> noise
#                  regen_last    : compress -> resample -> noise -> DAC
#                  regen_middle  : compress -> DAC -> resample -> noise
#                  double_regen  : DAC -> compress -> DAC -> resample
#
#                BER for BOTH Path A and Path B is tracked AFTER EVERY
#                HOP, not just at the end — the trajectory across the
#                chain is the actual result here, not a single number.
#                Uses the DEFENDER's real trained system (05's joint
#                checkpoint) throughout; no attacker model in this
#                experiment, no training — pure evaluation of an already-
#                trained system's behavior over a realistic lifecycle.
#
# PRE-FLIGHT SELF-TEST: confirms the E3 checkpoint reproduces its own
# known single-hop behavior (BER~0 benign for both paths) before running
# any multi-hop chain — isolates whether any chain-position effect
# observed later is real, not an artifact of a broken checkpoint load.
#
# INPUT:
#                  PROJECT_DIR (/content/drive/MyDrive/paper/HyperFrag/):
#                    dataset_e0.h5              — from 01_e0_dataextract.py
#                    e3_joint_checkpoint.pth    — from 05_e3_joint_path.py
#                                                 (REQUIRED)
#
# STEPS:
#                  Step 1  Download dataset_e0.h5 + e3_joint_checkpoint.pth
#                          from PROJECT_DIR
#                  Step 2  Load frozen EnCodec + DAC, slim self-test
#                  Step 3  Load E3's trained joint checkpoint, self-test
#                          single-hop behavior matches known results
#                  Step 4  For each of 105 held-out clips: embed jointly,
#                          then run all 5 chains, scoring Path A and Path
#                          B BER after every hop of every chain
#                  Step 5  Save per-chain, per-hop-position BER
#                          trajectories; generate trajectory figure
#
# OUTPUT FILES : (stored at /content/drive/MyDrive/paper/HyperFrag/)
#                  exp14_chain_of_custody_results.json
#                      {chain_name: {path_a: [ber_hop1..4], path_b: [ber_hop1..4]}}
#                  fig_14_01_chain_trajectories.png
#                      BER vs. hop position, one line per chain, separate
#                      panels for Path A and Path B — does regeneration's
#                      signature persist, and does chain position matter?
#
# GPU Required : YES
# Dependencies : torch, torchaudio, encodec, pydub, descript-audio-codec,
#                h5py, matplotlib, tqdm
#
